                          Brute Force
                          import java.util.Stack;

class MyQueue {
    private Stack<Integer> input;
    private Stack<Integer> temp;

    public MyQueue() {
        input = new Stack<>();
        temp = new Stack<>();
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        input.push(x);
    }

    public int pop() {
        // Move all elements to temp
        while (!input.isEmpty()) {
            temp.push(input.pop());
        }

        // Remove front element
        int front = temp.pop();

        // Move remaining elements back
        while (!temp.isEmpty()) {
            input.push(temp.pop());
        }

        return front;
    }

    public int peek() {
        // Move all elements to temp
        while (!input.isEmpty()) {
            temp.push(input.pop());
        }

        // Get front element
        int front = temp.peek();

        // Move elements back
        while (!temp.isEmpty()) {
            input.push(temp.pop());
        }

        return front;
    }

    public boolean empty() {
        return input.isEmpty();
    }
}


                          Intuition
Instead of transferring elements every time a pop() or peek() operation is performed, the optimized solution transfers elements only when necessary.
Two stacks are maintained:
input stack → stores newly inserted elements.
output stack → provides queue elements in FIFO order.
Every push() operation simply inserts the element into the input stack.
When pop() or peek() is called:
If the output stack already contains elements, use it directly.
If the output stack is empty, move all elements once from the input stack to the output stack.
This reversal makes the oldest inserted element appear on the top of the output stack, preserving queue order.
Subsequent pop() and peek() operations use the output stack without any additional transfers until it becomes empty again.


                    Time Complexity
push()	=> O(1)
pop() =>	O(n)
peek() =>	O(n)
empty() =>	O(1)
